<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  com.claude.capacity-alarm — row 13 M6. Every 10 min: is the box approaching its memory ceiling?

  WHY A JOB AT ALL: the row measured a real ceiling (~50 concurrent sessions on this 10-core/64 GiB
  box) and shipped scripts/capacity-alarm.sh to read it — but a built alarm that nothing invokes is
  inert, which is the failure this repo names as feature-durability-mechanism-not-memory. This is the
  thing that fires it.

  WHY IT IS CHEAP ENOUGH TO BE ALLOWED TO EXIST (this row is about NOT adding load): one vm_stat +
  one sysctl + one ps + one python3, measured ~100-200 ms per tick. At 600 s that is a duty cycle
  around 0.03% of one core. It stays cheap via Nice + LowPriorityIO, which yield to interactive
  sessions.

  PROCESSTYPE WAS "Background" AND THAT MADE THE SENSOR INERT WHEN IT MATTERED — measured, not
  theorised. After the 2026-07-30 02:18:05 panic-reboot this job did not emit a single sample for
  6 HOURS 24 MINUTES (last pre-panic row 09:05:40Z, next row 15:41:29Z; max inter-sample gap 23,749 s
  against a configured 600 s), because the box came up under a Spotlight reindex storm — load average
  140, mds at 90% CPU — and Darwin defers the Background band aggressively under exactly that load.
  A capacity sensor that stops sampling when the machine is busy is inert precisely when it is
  needed, and its silence is indistinguishable from a green reading.

  ProcessType is therefore "Adaptive": still not user-interactive, still Nice 10 + LowPriorityIO 1
  (a ~0.03% duty cycle cannot be the thing that loads a 10-core box), but no longer subject to
  Background-band deferral. Note the one-way-ratchet caveat from memory darwin-qos-band-mechanics:
  `nice` alone does NOT demote a process (PRI stays 31) — the demotion came from ProcessType, so
  this key is the correct and only lever here.

  RunAtLoad stays false and is NOT implicated in the gap above — a StartInterval job fires on its
  interval regardless; the 6h24m silence was deferral, not the absence of a load-time run.

  THE PATH MUST CARRY /usr/sbin — THREE RUNGS WERE SILENTLY DEAD WITHOUT IT. launchd hands the job
  PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin, but the wrapper below OVERWRITES it, and the original override
  omitted /usr/sbin. `sysctl` lives in /usr/sbin. So in every SCHEDULED run (never in a hand-run from
  an interactive shell, which is why it survived review) these three all failed open:
      rung 1 swap      SWAP_MB   <- sysctl vm.swapusage
      rung 3 pressure  PRESSURE  <- sysctl kern.memorystatus_vm_pressure_level
      rung 5 segments  SEG_PCT   <- sysctl vm.compressor_segment_limit
  Measured in the 17:25:59Z scheduled row: pressure_level=null, seg_pct=null, and — the dangerous
  one — swap_used_mb=0, because `${SWAP_MB:-0}` renders an UNREADABLE instrument as the healthy
  value 0, indistinguishable from "no swap in use". A dead rung that reports OK is worse than an
  absent rung. tests/capacity-alarm-launchd-path.bats now asserts every binary the script invokes is
  reachable on THIS plist's own PATH string, with the pre-fix PATH as the RED control.

  WHY StartInterval AND NOT a calendar entry: headroom is a continuous quantity, not a daily event.
  RunAtLoad is false — a login must not page about a condition that has not been sampled yet.

  IT NEVER REFUSES ANYTHING. The script reports and exits; it has no gate, no spawn veto, no sleep
  (R1). The landed capacity_gate() is the cautionary case: measured REFUSE 10/10 against real load,
  i.e. a permanent dispatch outage. See docs/plans/MACHINE_CAPACITY_V2.md §8.5.7.

  Pages to ~/.claude/autonomy/pages/capacity-alarm.page on WARN/ALARM, and REMOVES that page on
  OK/NO-DATA — one fixed slug, so a 10-minute cadence cannot accumulate pages, and a cleared
  condition stops being reported.

  C10: the OPERATOR loads it. Kill switches after load: CC_CAPACITY_ALARM=off (whole script),
  CC_CAP_PAGE=off (page channel only), or launchctl bootout.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.claude.capacity-alarm</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>-c</string>
        <string>export PATH="$HOME/.claude/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin"; exec "$HOME/.claude/scripts/capacity-alarm.sh" --quiet</string>
    </array>

    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>600</integer>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>

    <key>ProcessType</key>      <string>Adaptive</string>
    <key>LowPriorityIO</key>    <true/>
    <key>Nice</key>             <integer>10</integer>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>  <string>/Users/chrisren/.claude/logs/capacity-alarm.out.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/Users/chrisren/.claude/logs/capacity-alarm.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
